for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?

answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-hide-word
The hide word removes the body copy of a pin in reading mode.
Write it last on the first line, for example top hide or bottom
read hide. In reading mode the pin stays on screen and its block
is gone from the page body, so a reader sees it once. In writing
mode hide does nothing, the body copy always stays. Why: a reader
on a small screen does not need the same note twice, a writer
needs the whole page.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?

answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-book-structure
The structure blocks give a book its shape. A part groups chapters and sits above the chapter level. It holds its own title as first block and takes no number, so chapters keep counting across parts. An appendix is a chapter kept out of the body, numbered with letters A, B and C, and its sub-chapters A.1, A.2. A glossary is never typed, it is computed from the definition blocks, one row per term in sorted order, the way the index is computed from the term declarations. Why, a book needs groups above its chapters, a place for side material, and a list of its own terms with no second copy to keep in sync.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?

answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-edition-numbers
The edition command cuts a book into pages. Everything before the first chapter or part is front matter: the title, dedication, epigraph, abstract and acknowledgments. It is numbered with roman numerals, i, ii, iii. These blocks carry block numbers like any other block, so the edition command skips them by name when it looks for where the body starts. Why, otherwise the dedication would be taken for the first body block and the body page numbers would shift. The body then starts on a fresh page numbered 1, and the footer reads page 1 of N counting body pages only. Why, a printed book keeps its front matter out of the count so that page 1 is where the reading starts, and the index shows the same body numbers.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?

answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-edition-paper
The edition command takes --paper a6, a5, a4 or letter, which sets the page width and the lines per page, and --margin N, which keeps N blank columns on the left and right of every page. Why: a book on paper needs a known page size and room for the thumb. An explicit --width or --lines always wins over the paper, in any order. An unknown paper name stops with an error, so a typo never prints the wrong size.
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-research-blocks
The blocks claim, evidence, hypothesis and remark are for research. The first line is the name, as with example and exercise, and the rest is the body. They are numbered in their chapter like every content block. Why: research is claims and the support for them, so each part gets its own box and its own number. The quote block is a quotation, and its last line is the attribution, drawn after a dash. Why: a quote in the middle of a chapter needs its speaker, and an epigraph only works at the front of a book.
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-aside
The aside block is a small note drawn directly under the block it belongs to, indented so it reads as a note on that block. Write it right after its block. Why: everything is a box, so a side column is not needed, and the note stays next to the text it explains.
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-break
Any block takes a break declaration. The value before starts that block on a new page in the edition. The value keep never splits the block over two pages: when it does not fit the rest of the page, it moves whole to the next page. Why: a chapter should open on a fresh page, and an exercise with its space should stay in one piece on paper. The declaration changes only the edition, never the screen view. A block taller than one page is cut as before.
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-contracts-command
The contracts command is run as notation contracts FILE. It adds every missing contract and answer slot, writes them into the file, and prints only the open questions: the contract id, the block type and name it sits in, and the question. Why: a new page no longer has to be opened in the editor to get its contracts, and the printed list can be handed back to be answered. Answered questions are not listed, unless the flag --all is added: then every contract is listed and the answered ones are marked answered. The launcher aiu-contracts FILE runs the same command from any folder. Running it twice changes nothing.
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-a-pinned-block
A pinned block also stays in the page body, so it shows twice, unless the pin has the hide word.
That is on purpose: the pin keeps it in sight while the page
scrolls, and the body copy keeps the page complete. Pins show
their real block numbers, but a pin block takes no number of
its own, so it never shifts the numbers of chapters or blocks.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?

answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-a-formula-is
A formula is text the parser does not read, drawn in a frame
as written. A space holds one line, the number of blank lines
to leave: 8 if missing, 60 at most. The box keeps its blank
lines, so the edition leaves room on the page for hand work.
