answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-server-load
The server command load FOLDER indexes a folder like notation
index and also keeps the index in the memory of the running
server. Why: one index serves every package, so later commands
read it without a new walk. It answers the counts of files,
blocks and records, or one error line. Example: load on this
folder answers loaded 17 files 1443 blocks 2011 records.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the server load command and why.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-server-reads
The server reads the loaded index with four commands. links FILE
lists the links written in one file, by the path the index uses,
with target, relation and mode. backlinks NAME lists every link
that points to the block NAME, so when page 2 links to page 1,
page 1 shows it at once. Backlinks are computed on every ask and
never stored, so they cannot go stale. broken lists links whose
target does not exist. names lists every block name with its
file. Each answers one line, or an error when no index is loaded.
Example: backlinks p-b answers backlinks 1 a.aiu:6 reference.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the four server read commands and why.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-server-reload-git
The command reload reindexes the folder of the last load, so a
file changed by hand shows up without typing the folder again.
Why: the server never watches the disk. The command git FILE
reports the git repo that holds the file, found by walking up to
the nearest .git, whether user.name and user.email are set, and
whether that one file is clean or dirty. Why: every save is a
local git commit, so a missing name or email must be seen before
it breaks a save. Only the one file is checked, because a full
status of a large repo takes seconds. Example: git on a page
answers git REPO name yes email yes dirty.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains reload and git and why each exists.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-server-http
The server has a second door: HTTP on 127.0.0.1, port 8080 or
AIU_SERVER_PORT. Why: the website is wasm in a browser and a
browser cannot use the Unix socket. GET /index.db returns the
saved index of the last load. GET of any other path serves a
file from the website folder, and a path with two dots is
refused. POST /cmd runs the body as one command, through the same
handler as the socket, so every door answers alike. The stop
command is refused over HTTP. Example: POST /cmd with the body
names answers the block names of the loaded folder.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the HTTP door and why it exists.
answer end
paragraph end
paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-server-websocket
The HTTP port also speaks WebSocket. Why: the mind map must
update while you edit, and a browser cannot wait for the server
with plain requests. A GET with the Upgrade: websocket header on
any path switches the connection to WebSocket. Each text message
is one command, answered through the same handler as the socket,
and stop is refused. After every load or reload the server pushes
the text changed to all connected clients. Dead connections are
dropped, so one slow client never blocks the rest. Example:
connect, send ping, get pong; run reload and get changed.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the WebSocket door and why it exists.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-notation-wasm
The notation crate also builds to WebAssembly. Why: the website
runs the same parser and index code in the browser, so it reads
index.db with no second implementation to keep in step. The
command ecosystem live aiu-notation-wasm runs cargo build for the
target wasm32-unknown-unknown and links notation.wasm into the
package bin folder. No source change was needed: file and
environment calls compile for wasm and only fail when called, and
the website uses the in-memory parts only. Example: after the
command, bin/notation.wasm exists in the aiu-notation-wasm prefix.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the wasm build of the crate and why it exists.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-website-bridge
The website folder holds a small wasm bridge. Why: the browser
must load the saved index into the same Store the server uses,
with no second reader to keep in step. The bridge crate uses the
notation crate by path and exports alloc, free_buf, load_index and
block_count. A loader of about 10 lines of JS fetches bridge.wasm
and index.db, copies the bytes into wasm memory and calls
load_index, which returns the block count. The command ecosystem
live aiu-website builds it. Example: after load FOLDER, the page
index.html on the HTTP door prints blocks followed by the count.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the wasm bridge that loads the index in the browser.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-website-commands
The wasm bridge can send commands to the server and read the
answers, with no interface at all. Why: the website must use the
same commands as every other package, so it needs no second API.
The loader opens a WebSocket to the HTTP port and gives wasm one
function, send. The wasm export command sends a command text
through it. Each answer comes back as a message, the loader hands
it to on_message, and answer_ptr and answer_len read the last
answer. Example: command with the text ping makes the last answer
pong.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains how the wasm bridge sends commands and reads answers.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-write-commands
The server writes files with three commands: write, delete and
move. Why: every writer must change files the same way, and git
must keep every state so nothing is ever lost. The first line is
the command and the path. For write, the text follows on the next
lines. For move, the target path is the second line. Because they
need a newline, they work over HTTP and WebSocket and answer an
error on the Unix socket. Each command is one local git commit in
the nearest repo. Delete and move first commit the file as it is,
and refuse if that commit fails. A missing repo or git is reported
and never blocks the write. The index is not updated by a write
yet, the reload command does that. Example: delete a file, then
restore it from the commit before.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Yes. It explains the write commands and the commit each one makes.
answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-the-mindmap-plan
The mind map has its own plan file, ecosystem-mindmap-plan.aiu.
Why: the map is drawn by wasm from the server index and its rules
must be written before any drawing exists. The plan fixes that
every file is a box with its title, that boxes sit inside boxes,
that the map always opens on the master view with the links as
connections, and that expanding a box by key comes after the first
drawing works. Layout files decide where boxes sit. A layout block
holds card blocks and box blocks, each with a target and a place
of x y, and a caption for the description. The page reads every
layout and draws them combined. See layout-plan.aiu.
Far zoom shows only box titles, close zoom shows cards. Why: a
big map stays readable. The zoomcut setting is the switch.
Far zoom also shrinks outer boxes to fit their titles. Why: more
of the map fits on one phone screen.
Far boxes are sized from their text, columns and rows packed.
Why: no empty space between boxes at far zoom.
Zoom eases and near and far layers crossfade.
Zoom cut, fade band, ease and wheel are settings in the TUI.
Why: every zoom feel can be tuned without code.
Why: no sudden jumps when you zoom in or out.
Drawing: the wasm lays out every box and calls rect, line and
text, three functions the loader provides, so the browser only
paints. Why: all logic stays in Rust. Example: scrolling draws
only the boxes near the screen.
contract start
id: q94fc72
question: Does the paragraph make one point?
contract end
answer start
for: q94fc72
asked: Does the paragraph make one point?
Partly. It explains why the mind map has its own plan file, then how boxes are placed and drawn.
answer end
paragraph end





chapter end

